### What is if-else?

A single `if` statement either executes code or skips it. But often we have two mutually exclusive choices where **exactly one** must run:
- A student either **passes** or **fails**.
- An integer is either **even** or **odd**.
- A user's PIN is either **valid** or **invalid**.

This is implemented using the `if-else` construct:

```java
if (condition) {
    // Branch A: Runs ONLY if condition is TRUE
} else {
    // Branch B: Runs ONLY if condition is FALSE
}
// Resumes common execution
```

**Key Guarantee:** In an `if-else` pair, exactly one of the two blocks will execute—never both, and never neither.

---

### The Danger of Omitting Curly Braces `{ }`

Java allows omitting curly braces if a branch contains only **one single statement**:

```java
// Legal, but DANGEROUS:
if (score >= 40)
    System.out.println("Passed");
else
    System.out.println("Failed");
```

#### Why omitting braces leads to catastrophic bugs:
If a developer later adds a second line without adding braces:

```java
// BUG! Apple "goto fail" style bug:
if (isAdmin)
    grantAccess();
    logAdminActivity(); // ALWAYS RUNS, even if isAdmin is FALSE!
```
Indentation in Java is purely visual for humans—the compiler only attaches the **very first statement** to the `if`. The second line executes unconditionally!

> [!TIP]
> **Industry Standard (Google & Oracle Java Style Guide)**:
> Always include curly braces `{ ... }`, even for single-line statements. It prevents silent logic bugs when modifying code later.

---

### Classic Real-World Examples

#### 1. Even or Odd Number:
```java
if (num % 2 == 0) {
    System.out.println(num + " is Even.");
} else {
    System.out.println(num + " is Odd.");
}
```

#### 2. Pass or Fail:
```java
if (marks >= 40) {
    System.out.println("Result: PASS");
} else {
    System.out.println("Result: FAIL (Needs Retest)");
}
```

